  "text": "Register an artist. Consent is recorded before anything can be generated \u2014 no consent, no generation, and the roster says so.\n\nBuild the identity once: reference frames across angles, the words the model is told, and the ones it must never render.\n\nAttach a song. It's measured once \u2014 tempo, sections, hook window. And the app refuses a tempo that arrives without the method that produced it.\n\nThen generate. A performance clip. A press still. The artist announcing a show, straight to camera. Or a backdrop built to be copied, so a fan who sees one knows what their own version looks like.\n\nThe shape of an asset used to be a commit. Now it's a row \u2014 prompt, negatives, length, how much of the artist it needs. Adding a format is a document, not a deploy.\n\nEach clip is two models from two vendors in one run: the face settled first as a still, four cents, and that frame becomes the first frame of the video, eighty. Priced before you buy it \u2014 and past your ceiling the run is refused before a single call is submitted. A submitted step is a charged step.\n\nBuild it once, and the second video is cheap. Cost per attempt is the only lever left.\n\nHere's what runs it. AWS handles the logic. Backblaze B2 holds the bytes \u2014 and the proof.\n\nThat split is why this system has no database. A label stores documents, not rows \u2014 an artist, its measurements, a finished kit \u2014 so they live in a B2 bucket. Eleven services became five; idle cost, under a dollar a month.\n\nB2 does four jobs.\n\nIt's the upload path \u2014 masters go browser straight to bucket, never touching our server.\n\nIt's the catalog. Every run writes under tenant, date, run ID.\n\nIt's the receipt. Every asset lands beside a manifest naming the provider, model and prompt behind every step. A kit that can't prove how it was made never reaches ready.\n\nAnd it's the disclosure. Download a clip and that manifest travels inside the file, while the stored object stays byte-identical \u2014 so the hash still means something. Drop it into verify and it says exactly how it was made. Nothing is looked up. The proof is in the bytes.\n\nYou cannot buy a hit. You can only lower the cost of trying \u2014 and be honest about what came out.\n\nBring us one artist and one song.",
